    summary: `Medgar Evers is often remembered at the moment of his murder, lying in his driveway in Jackson, Mississippi, while his family was inside the house. That ending is devastating, but it is not the whole life. Evers was a World War II veteran, college student, insurance worker, husband, father, and the NAACP's first field secretary in Mississippi. His work was local, relentless, and dangerous: voter registration, school desegregation, boycotts, investigations of racial violence, and support for people whose names did not make national news. He was not waiting for the movement to arrive in Mississippi. He was building it under threat every day. His story matters because civil rights depended on organizers who knew the risks and stayed anyway.`,
