    tags: ['civil-rights', 'black-lives-matter', 'voting-rights', 'policing', 'organizing'],
    summary: `Modern civil rights leadership is not one person at one podium. It is a network of organizers, lawyers, scholars, voters, survivors, local groups, digital strategists, clergy, students, families, and policy workers fighting over policing, voting, prisons, education, housing, health, and memory. Black Lives Matter, founded by Alicia Garza, Patrisse Cullors, and Opal Tometi after Trayvon Martin's killer was acquitted, became one visible language for a wider movement. Bryan Stevenson, Kimberle Crenshaw, Stacey Abrams, Darnella Frazier, and countless local organizers show different kinds of leadership. This page matters because civil rights is not a finished chapter. It is being argued, filmed, litigated, organized, funded, attacked, misunderstood, and remade in public.`,
